WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,606
A practical network hardening routine starts with inventory and ownership.

2
00:00:05,606 --> 00:00:17,336
Know the assets, subnets, VLANs, cloud networks, wireless networks, services, management interfaces, third-party connections, and critical dependencies.

3
00:00:17,336 --> 00:00:22,598
Assign owners so hardening decisions have context and accountability.

4
00:00:22,598 --> 00:00:34,500
Map the paths that matter most, including user access, application dependencies, administrative access, DNS, DHCP, NTP, monitoring, backup, and remote access.

5
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,174
Next, reduce unnecessary exposure.

6
00:00:37,174 --> 00:00:47,869
Disable or retire what is not needed, remove obsolete rules, restrict public exposure, protect management interfaces, and document exceptions.

7
00:00:47,869 --> 00:00:57,788
Segment networks by purpose, risk, and sensitivity so compromise or failure in one area does not automatically affect every other area.

8
00:00:57,788 --> 00:01:04,171
Keep segmentation understandable enough that teams can troubleshoot and maintain it.

9
00:01:04,171 --> 00:01:07,793
Then protect administration and harden devices.

10
00:01:07,793 --> 00:01:22,111
Use unique accounts, least privilege, multi-factor authentication, controlled access paths, management networks, bastion hosts where appropriate, logging, and protected emergency access.

11
00:01:22,111 --> 00:01:32,375
Apply approved baselines to routers, switches, firewalls, wireless controllers, VPN gateways, DNS, DHCP, NTP, and other shared services.

12
00:01:32,375 --> 00:01:36,256
Back up configurations and stage changes carefully.

13
00:01:36,256 --> 00:01:39,879
Finally, patch, monitor, validate, and improve.

14
00:01:39,879 --> 00:01:45,916
Use maintenance windows, testing, change approval, backups, and rollback plans.

15
00:01:45,916 --> 00:01:48,417
Track exceptions and review them.

16
00:01:48,417 --> 00:01:57,129
Monitor logs, alerts, configuration drift, rule sets, access, availability, DNS, certificates, and failed changes.

17
00:01:57,129 --> 00:02:10,843
Network hardening is practical risk reduction through disciplined operations: inventory, classify, reduce exposure, segment, control access, patch, monitor, validate, and improve.
